  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nA stout 597 dm ridge of high pressure remains in control across the \nFA this afternoon with near record to record temperatures ranging \nfrom the mid 90s to near 100 degrees. Dewpoints will remain more \nelevated through Friday around 70 degrees, translating to daily\nheat index values of 103-109 degrees. While the ridge begins to\nbreakdown over the weekend, temperatures will remain 10-15 \ndegrees above normal. With a backdoor frontal boundary making \nits approach Friday, the only noteworthy change will be slightly\nlower dewpoints over the weekend in the mid 60s. Despite the \ndrier air, heat index values will remain around 100 degrees \nthrough Sunday. For these reasons, a Heat Advisory remains in \neffect until Sunday evening as the late season dangerous heat \nwave prevails. \n\nMeanwhile, the aformentioned sfc boundary to the north will be close \nenough to trigger a 20% chance of isolated showers and thunderstorms \nthis afternoon and Friday afternoon and evening, mainly along I-64. \nCoverage will be sparse at best, but a greater risk arrives on \nMonday as a much stronger cold front approaches the FA. Rain \nchances peak at 30-40% chance Monday afternoon into Monday night\non the NBM, as there is currently some uncertainty in the \ntiming of the front. The good news is relief arrives by Tuesday \nwith temperatures turning more seasonable, but rain chances may \nlinger. There is however a large spread on the NBM distribution \nas the ECMWF tries to build the ridge back in while a trough \nprevails more on the GFS with a unsettled pattern for the latter\nhalf of next week.",
